$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM B) Oxidation to 1,3,5,-Triones. A typical procedure for the formation of
1,3,5-triones involved addition of 4.5 equiv of 5 M DMSO/CH2Cl2 solution
to 3 equiv of a 0.4 M solution of oxalyl chloride CH2Cl2 at -78 °C. After
15 minutes a 0.4 M solution of a isomeric mixture of β ,β
'-dihydroxyketones at -78 °C was transferred to the activated DMSO
solution via cannula. After 15 minutes at -78 °C, 10 equiv of
triethylamine was added and the reaction mixture stirred for an
additional 15 minutes at -78 °C. The reaction was warmed to room
temperature and stirred for 5 minutes, at which time it was quenched with
1M NaHSO4 and diluted with 20% CH2Cl2/hexane solution. The organic layer
was separated and washed twice with 1M NaHSO4 and twice with water. The
resulting organic layer was dried over Na2SO4 and concentrated to provide
a tautomeric mixture of 1,3,5-triones. These were purified by
chromatography on silica (flash chromatography followed by preparative
HPLC, if necessary) with hexane/ethyl acetate solutions. Yield 85 %.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
